---
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
import DocLink from "../../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Example from "../../../components/Example/index"
---

<section>
  <h2 id="theming">Theming</h2>
  <p>
    The basic idea is to pick one hue and chroma, and derive a 16-step palette
    from them. <code>theme.css</code> sets <code>--palette-hue</code> and <code
      >--palette-chroma</code
    >, and <code>core/palette.css</code> turns them into a source color, <code
      >--palette-source</code
    >, and the <code>--color-1</code> to <code>--color-16</code> steps.
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`:where(html) {
  --palette-hue: 264;
  --palette-chroma: 0.5;
  --palette-hue-rotate-by: 0;
}`}
    lang="css"
  />

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>--palette-hue</code></strong> is a hue angle in degrees. Open
      Props' <code>--hue-*</code> tokens work here. The default is green in light
      mode and blue in dark mode.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>--palette-chroma</code></strong> scales the saturation, from
      {" "}<code>0</code> (gray) to <code>1</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>--palette-hue-rotate-by</code></strong> is a separate knob for
      per-step warm/cool drift, in degrees.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    You can also set <code>--palette-source</code> directly (it must be an <code
      >oklch()</code
    > color). Set it on <code>:root</code>, or on an element with <code
      >.ui-palette</code
    > to re-theme that part of the page. Anywhere else it has no effect: the palette
    is computed where <code>core/palette.css</code> declares it and only inherited
    from there. The same goes for <code>--palette-hue</code>, <code
      >--palette-chroma</code
    > and <code>--palette-hue-rotate-by</code>.
  </p>

  <p>This is how the severity classes get their colors:</p>

  <Code
    code={`:where(.ui-warning) {
  --palette-source: oklch(0.58 0.21 var(--hue-orange));
}`}
    lang="css"
  />

  <p>
    Every token with its default is listed on the{" "}
    <DocLink href="/guide/theme-tokens">theme tokens</DocLink> page.
  </p>

  <h3 id="theme-generator"><code>theme.css</code> generator</h3>
  <p>
    There's a WIP visual editor for editing <code>theme.css</code> - <DocLink
      href="/guide/theme-generator">try it out</DocLink
    >!
  </p>

  <h3 id="theming-scopes">Scopes</h3>
  <p>
    <code>.ui-light</code> and <code>.ui-dark</code> force a color scheme. Put them
    on <code>html</code> to control the whole page, or on any element to force a subtree.
    {" "}<code>.ui-palette</code> re-derives the palette and all color tokens from
    the knobs set on that element, so one page can carry several brands.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`<aside class="ui-dark">Always dark</aside>

<section class="ui-palette" style="--palette-hue: 30">
  <button class="ui-button ui-primary ui-filled">Orange brand</button>
</section>`}
    lang="html"
  />
</section>

<section>
  <h2 id="motion">Motion</h2>
  <p>
    Use the <code>--motion</code> variable to turn motion on or off. The default value
    is <code>1</code>. If a user has <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code> enabled,
    {" "}<code>--motion</code> will be set to <code>0</code> by default.
  </p>

  <h3 id="motion-global">Global classes</h3>
  <p>
    Adding these utility classes to the <code>html</code> element will override the
    OS preference.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>.ui-motion-off</code>: sets <code>--motion: 0</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>.ui-motion-on</code>: sets <code>--motion: 1</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>.ui-motion-debug</code>: sets <code>--motion: 10</code> (slows down transitions
      10x).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Code code={`<html lang="en" class="ui-motion-debug">`} lang="html" />

  <h3 id="motion-local">Local overrides</h3>
  <p>
    Components use a local <code>--_motion</code> variable that allows you to disable
    motion for each component individually if you want.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`<button class="ui-button" style="--_motion: 0">
  Instant interaction
</button>`}
    lang="html"
  />

  <p>
    Additionally, this is how you could include <code>--motion</code> in your CSS:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`transition: transform calc(var(--duration) * var(--motion, 1)) var(--ease);`}
    lang="css"
  />
</section>

<section>
  <h2 id="contrast">Contrast</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>--contrast</code> variable is <code>normal</code> by default. If a user
    has <code>prefers-contrast: more</code> enabled, it is set to <code
      >more</code
    >, and a style query raises the contrast of muted text, borders, field
    borders, the primary color, intent colors and the focus ring. Components
    with translucent text, such as Tabs, keyboard hints and inline code, follow
    along.
  </p>
  <p>
    Try it with the <strong>High contrast</strong> switch in the theme config drawer.
  </p>

  <Example row showCode={false}>
    <div class="contrast-demo">
      <section>
        <p class="ui-caption">Default</p>
        <label class="ui-text-field">
          <span class="ui-label">Email</span>
          <span class="ui-field">
            <input type="email" placeholder="name@example.com" />
          </span>
          <span class="ui-end-text">We'll never share it.</span>
        </label>
        <button class="ui-button ui-primary ui-outlined">Subscribe</button>
      </section>
      <section class="ui-contrast-more">
        <p class="ui-caption">.ui-contrast-more</p>
        <label class="ui-text-field">
          <span class="ui-label">Email</span>
          <span class="ui-field">
            <input type="email" placeholder="name@example.com" />
          </span>
          <span class="ui-end-text">We'll never share it.</span>
        </label>
        <button class="ui-button ui-primary ui-outlined">Subscribe</button>
      </section>
    </div>
  </Example>

  <h3 id="contrast-classes">Classes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>.ui-contrast-more</code>: sets <code>--contrast: more</code>. Put it
      on{" "}
      <code>html</code> for the whole page, or on any element to raise the contrast
      of its children.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>.ui-contrast-normal</code>: sets <code>--contrast: normal</code>.
      Put it on <code>html</code> to ignore the OS preference.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Code code={`<html lang="en" class="ui-contrast-more">`} lang="html" />

  <h3 id="contrast-custom">Custom values</h3>
  <p>
    Style queries match against the parent element, so the overrides are set on
    <code>body</code> instead of <code>html</code>. They replace any value you
    set on <code>html</code> for the same tokens. To tune them, write your own style
    query:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`@container style(--contrast: more) {
  :where(body) {
    --border-color: var(--text-muted);
    --field-border-color: var(--border-color);
  }
}`}
    lang="css"
  />
  <p>
    Tokens that reference an overridden token, like <code
      >--field-border-color</code
    > above, have to be set again in the same rule.
  </p>

  <h3 id="forced-colors">Forced colors</h3>
  <p>
    When an OS contrast theme forces its own palette (<code
      >forced-colors: active</code
    >), components switch to system colors so their state stays visible.
    Selected tabs, toggles and list items use <code>SelectedItem</code>,
    switches, ranges, progress bars and dividers are drawn with <code
      >CanvasText</code
    >, and focused fields get a <code>Highlight</code> outline. There is nothing to
    configure.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .contrast-demo {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--size-4);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
    inline-size: 100%;

    section {
      align-items: start;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: var(--size-3);
    }

    .ui-text-field {
      inline-size: 100%;
    }
  }
</style>
